  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ending Child Labour/Migration in Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh is one of the most densely-populated countries in the world. Although the national statistical authority held its last population and housing census in 2011, it is estimated that by the end of 2020 the population stood at 164.7 million with a population density of approximately 1265 persons per square kilometre. 
Economically, Bangladesh reached lower-middle income status designation in 2015 having made rapid social and economic progress in recent decades. The pace of poverty reduction has slowed in recent years, even as growth has accelerated, particularly in urban areas. Addressing climate risks is increasingly urgent to ensure sustainable economic development of the country. The Global Climate Risk Index ranks Bangladesh as the world&#x2019;s 7th most affected country over the period 1999- 2018.
In such a socio-economic and climate change context, labour migration is a survival strategy for hundreds of thousands of people in Bangladesh. Male children who had dropped out of school were lined up for migration at an early age without skills and proper preparation. Many female children who got married at an early age in the absence of their mothers are also trapped into the migration cycle to earn for their husbands shortly after their marriage. 
In order to support vulnerable children the project aims to:
*Children and adolescent girls are educated and equipped with knowledge and life skills for self-care, self-protection and to act as change makers in their communities.
*Families and local stakeholders are sensitised and act to prevent incidents of child labour/migration</narrative>
  </description>
